# Family A15: Kafka nack strategies (driverkit/nack). See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./driverkit/nack
BENCH=BenchmarkKafkaNack
REF=blockpartition
AUTO_KEY=nack
ADR=13
TITLE="Kafka nack strategy (A15)"
# The strategies live in the root module rather than in drivers/kafka because
# the decision is broker-independent and because hack/bakeoff.sh runs one
# `go test` in PKG from the repository root: a root-module `go test` cannot
# reach a nested driver module's package (assumption A24.7, and family A29's
# own failure). The Kafka driver binds a kind and applies its Plan.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="throughput vs failure rate 0.1/1/10%; ordering violations; lag -> none silently chosen; default block-partition under SafeOnly"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: driverkit/nack has no IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX
# branch, and its arms are §9.1's own three failure rates plus the pure
# decision.
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="failrate=0.1 failrate=1 failrate=10"
# ticks/record is throughput, reorder/record is "ordering violations" and lag
# is "lag", all three from the reference simulation's virtual clock, whose cost
# model is documented at the top of driverkit/nack/sim_test.go. ns/op is what
# the strategy's own code costs and is not the criterion: the interesting cost
# of a nack strategy is what it does to the partition, not what Plan costs to
# call.
DECIDED_ON="ticks/record reorder/record lag ns/op"
# The default is fixed by §9.1 independently of the measurement -- "none
# silently chosen; default block-partition under SafeOnly" -- so a campaign
# that finds retry topics faster does NOT move Auto. What it records is the
# margin a caller pays for ordering, which is why every arm is measured
# anyway.
